1
00:00:00,241 --> 00:00:02,349
This small scene is our starting point:

2
00:00:02,374 --> 00:00:05,911
a tree, a rock, and space to walk between them.

3
00:00:06,086 --> 00:00:08,493
We're going to build it inside Genshin Impact,

4
00:00:08,561 --> 00:00:10,775
starting from the button that opens the editor.

5
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:13,223
You don't need any previous editing experience.

6
00:00:18,395 --> 00:00:19,329
Here's our route.

7
00:00:19,376 --> 00:00:20,353
Find the editor.

8
00:00:20,443 --> 00:00:21,804
Learn its main controls.

9
00:00:21,893 --> 00:00:23,638
Place two scenery objects.

10
00:00:23,771 --> 00:00:26,732
Change their position, direction, and size.

11
00:00:26,928 --> 00:00:29,356
Then save and walk through the scene as a player.

12
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:31,563
Pause whenever you want to try it yourself.

13
00:00:31,684 --> 00:00:34,027
Use the PC version with a keyboard and mouse,

14
00:00:34,074 --> 00:00:37,291
and an account where Miliastra Wonderland is already unlocked.

15
00:00:37,487 --> 00:00:39,829
These are English menus on the America server.

16
00:00:39,919 --> 00:00:41,782
Start in the normal open world.

17
00:00:41,948 --> 00:00:44,205
Press Escape to open the Paimon Menu.

18
00:00:44,252 --> 00:00:47,234
Scroll down until you see the Miliastra Wonderland section.

19
00:00:47,324 --> 00:00:48,855
Choose My Miliastra.

20
00:00:49,073 --> 00:00:52,383
The nearby Wonderland button is for browsing and playing stages.

21
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:55,698
My Miliastra is the route to your own saves and editing.

22
00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:00,641
If the section is missing, check your game's unlock requirements
before continuing.

23
00:01:00,993 --> 00:01:02,332
This is the save page.

24
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:03,783
Stay on Local Save.

25
00:01:03,894 --> 00:01:07,175
A save contains your stage's settings and placed content.

26
00:01:07,393 --> 00:01:09,842
Reopen an existing project with Edit.

27
00:01:09,995 --> 00:01:13,711
For this lesson, choose Create New Save at the bottom right.

28
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:17,664
Give it a clear name, such as Woodland Workshop Episode One.

29
00:01:17,775 --> 00:01:19,491
Keep your other projects as they are.

30
00:01:19,824 --> 00:01:22,199
The next window asks for a stage mode.

31
00:01:22,365 --> 00:01:25,796
The official guide explains that Beyond Mode uses Manekins,

32
00:01:25,864 --> 00:01:29,316
while Classic Mode lets players bring their Teyvat characters.

33
00:01:29,619 --> 00:01:34,019
This series uses Beyond Mode so we can build on the same setup in
later lessons.

34
00:01:34,205 --> 00:01:38,489
Select Beyond Mode, keep the other options as shown, then Confirm.

35
00:01:38,685 --> 00:01:40,878
Wait for the empty editing scene to appear.

36
00:01:40,946 --> 00:01:43,378
A blank field is enough for this first exercise.

37
00:01:44,848 --> 00:01:47,601
Let's identify the workspace before changing anything.

38
00:01:47,846 --> 00:01:51,063
The large middle area is the scene: the space you're building.

39
00:01:51,302 --> 00:01:54,070
The Place panel on the left supplies things to put in it.

40
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:57,783
Selecting something in the scene opens its Details panel on the
right.

41
00:01:58,001 --> 00:02:01,709
The menu button at the upper left opens the editor's System Menu,

42
00:02:01,798 --> 00:02:05,271
which contains saving, stage settings, and testing.

43
00:02:05,468 --> 00:02:09,197
Escape now opens that editor menu, because we're inside the
editor.

44
00:02:09,572 --> 00:02:12,939
Practise the editor camera using the finished pair as a reference.

45
00:02:13,114 --> 00:02:16,138
Click empty ground so you're not typing into a number box.

46
00:02:16,313 --> 00:02:19,372
Hold the right mouse button and move the mouse to look around.

47
00:02:19,539 --> 00:02:22,564
Keep it held and briefly press W to move forward.

48
00:02:22,653 --> 00:02:24,441
Release W and stop.

49
00:02:24,531 --> 00:02:26,617
Scroll backward to pull the view away.

50
00:02:26,749 --> 00:02:28,110
Watch the landmarks.

51
00:02:28,243 --> 00:02:29,893
This changes your editing view.

52
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:33,651
It does not move the objects or configure the player's starting
position.

53
00:02:36,984 --> 00:02:40,820
If that movement feels too fast, open the gear at the upper right.

54
00:02:40,952 --> 00:02:42,100
This is Quick Settings.

55
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:45,755
Camera Horizontal Speed controls movement through the editing
view.

56
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:49,133
In this example, I lower it from twenty-five to ten.

57
00:02:49,329 --> 00:02:51,885
That gives us smaller, easier movements.

58
00:02:52,017 --> 00:02:55,832
It changes the editor camera's speed, not the player's walking
speed.

59
00:02:55,985 --> 00:02:58,857
Close the settings and try one short movement again.

60
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:02,079
The active top tab is Entity Placement.

61
00:03:02,238 --> 00:03:04,451
The official guide describes it as the place

62
00:03:04,456 --> 00:03:07,098
to create scene entities and edit their data.

63
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:10,682
An entity is an individual item placed in your scene.

64
00:03:10,814 --> 00:03:13,380
We'll use Static Units for the tree and rock.

65
00:03:13,631 --> 00:03:18,192
Dynamic objects support gameplay features such as components and
node graphs.

66
00:03:18,282 --> 00:03:22,356
For this lesson, our scenery only needs placement and Transform
settings.

67
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:23,951
Let's place the tree.

68
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:28,901
In Place, expand Prefabricated, then Static Units, then Tree.

69
00:03:29,097 --> 00:03:30,266
Click an Arbor card.

70
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:34,319
The card is a library source; the outlined tree is the item we've
placed.

71
00:03:34,473 --> 00:03:36,885
Your initial location may differ with your view.

72
00:03:37,048 --> 00:03:39,037
Check that the right panel says Arbor.

73
00:03:39,267 --> 00:03:41,673
Open Transform in that Details panel.

74
00:03:41,827 --> 00:03:45,172
The official guide defines it as the object's geometric settings:

75
00:03:45,198 --> 00:03:47,786
Location, Rotate, and Zoom.

76
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:51,423
Begin with Location, which gives its position in world
coordinates.

77
00:03:51,577 --> 00:03:55,668
X and Z move across this flat field; Y controls height.

78
00:03:55,801 --> 00:04:00,307
Click the X number, select its old value, type eight, then press
Enter.

79
00:04:00,428 --> 00:04:04,370
Set Y to zero and Z to sixteen, applying each field.

80
00:04:04,524 --> 00:04:06,162
The tree may leave the current view.

81
00:04:06,188 --> 00:04:07,970
Use the camera to find it again.

82
00:04:08,138 --> 00:04:10,395
Turning the camera changes screen directions,

83
00:04:10,528 --> 00:04:13,147
while the world-coordinate values stay the same.

84
00:04:16,505 --> 00:04:19,317
Add a rock through Static Units, then Rock.

85
00:04:19,449 --> 00:04:22,709
Choose an uneven shape with a recognisable long side.

86
00:04:22,863 --> 00:04:26,995
Click its card, then check the outline and the name Rock on the
right.

87
00:04:27,082 --> 00:04:31,686
These checks matter: every value you type applies to the currently
selected item.

88
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:34,182
Clicking empty ground cancels the selection,

89
00:04:34,336 --> 00:04:37,353
so select the object again if its Details panel disappears.

90
00:04:37,772 --> 00:04:41,544
Under Rotate, enter ninety in Y and press Enter.

91
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:45,171
Rotation changes direction while the location stays the same.

92
00:04:45,324 --> 00:04:47,325
Ninety degrees is a quarter-turn.

93
00:04:47,458 --> 00:04:51,982
Watch the long side of the rock turn, and compare its outline
before and after.

94
00:04:52,162 --> 00:04:56,062
This is easier to recognise on an uneven shape than on something
round.

95
00:04:56,517 --> 00:04:58,454
The next group is named Zoom.

96
00:04:58,650 --> 00:05:01,654
In this panel, Zoom means object scale.

97
00:05:01,722 --> 00:05:03,985
One represents the original size.

98
00:05:04,123 --> 00:05:07,617
Zero point five makes a dimension half its original size.

99
00:05:07,792 --> 00:05:12,673
Enter zero point five in X, apply it, then do the same for Y and
Z.

100
00:05:12,784 --> 00:05:15,348
Changing one axis changes the proportions.

101
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:17,299
When all three values match,

102
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:21,032
the object keeps its original proportions at a smaller size.

103
00:05:21,378 --> 00:05:25,064
Here's the actual one-axis result beside the three-axis result.

104
00:05:27,321 --> 00:05:29,919
Let's apply the same controls to finish our tree.

105
00:05:30,009 --> 00:05:31,370
Select Arbor again.

106
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:41,083
For its size, enter one point two in all three Zoom fields.

107
00:05:41,243 --> 00:05:43,649
That's twenty percent larger in each dimension.

108
00:05:43,803 --> 00:05:46,593
Apply each field and check the tree in the scene.

109
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:51,671
Select the rock and set Location.

110
00:05:55,835 --> 00:05:58,924
The objects now mark opposite sides of an open approach.

111
00:05:59,078 --> 00:06:00,332
Bring both into view.

112
00:06:00,401 --> 00:06:02,177
Leave the middle clear for walking.

113
00:06:02,326 --> 00:06:04,920
Later, adjust the spacing for your own scene.

114
00:06:05,185 --> 00:06:09,297
A recovery example: I change one tree scale value too far,

115
00:06:09,408 --> 00:06:11,516
then use Undo in the upper toolbar.

116
00:06:11,627 --> 00:06:13,784
Undo reverses a recent operation.

117
00:06:13,986 --> 00:06:18,014
Watch the field return to one point two, then read the values
back.

118
00:06:18,210 --> 00:06:22,003
If you've selected the wrong object, stop typing and check its
name.

119
00:06:22,178 --> 00:06:25,505
Small, deliberate changes are easier to correct.

120
00:06:25,768 --> 00:06:26,809
Save your work.

121
00:06:26,878 --> 00:06:31,161
Open the upper-left System Menu and click the disk icon, Quick
Save.

122
00:06:31,315 --> 00:06:33,103
Wait for Saved successfully.

123
00:06:33,235 --> 00:06:35,876
Saving writes the current stage data to your project.

124
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:38,470
Test Play checks the scene from inside the game.

125
00:06:38,636 --> 00:06:40,949
Click Test Play in the System Menu.

126
00:06:41,094 --> 00:06:43,735
The official guide says this runs validation and

127
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:46,679
prepares the test data before opening the test settings.

128
00:06:46,897 --> 00:06:49,922
Here, the navmesh prompt offers Direct Upload.

129
00:06:50,054 --> 00:06:53,219
Choose it for this simple example and let preparation finish.

130
00:06:53,363 --> 00:06:57,796
In Test Play Options, check Player One and English as the test
language.

131
00:06:58,099 --> 00:07:01,764
Use the default Manekin for this lesson, then click Start Game.

132
00:07:01,896 --> 00:07:03,570
Loading is shortened in the video.

133
00:07:03,845 --> 00:07:06,882
This is an editor test, separate from making a public release.

134
00:07:12,386 --> 00:07:13,945
Here's our scene at player height.

135
00:07:13,965 --> 00:07:17,651
Hold W to walk toward the opening, then release it and stop.

136
00:07:17,805 --> 00:07:19,985
Move the mouse to look at both landmarks.

137
00:07:20,084 --> 00:07:21,829
Walk again while turning the view.

138
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:24,560
Check three things: can you approach the tree,

139
00:07:24,607 --> 00:07:27,909
can you pass through the gap, and can you tell where to go next?

140
00:07:28,063 --> 00:07:30,864
A high editor view makes distances look different.

141
00:07:31,072 --> 00:07:34,535
Walking the route gives you a better basis for deciding what to
change.

142
00:07:34,699 --> 00:07:39,409
To return, press Escape for the Test Play controls and choose
Abandon Challenge.

143
00:07:39,478 --> 00:07:41,329
The result may say Incomplete.

144
00:07:41,483 --> 00:07:43,767
Choose Exit to return to the editor.

145
00:07:43,968 --> 00:07:46,673
The guide calls this a non-standard test result.

146
00:07:46,784 --> 00:07:49,233
We're checking scenery, so that's expected here.

147
00:07:49,302 --> 00:07:53,056
Make one change if needed, Quick Save, and test its effect again.

148
00:07:53,187 --> 00:07:56,660
You can tell the camera view from an object's Transform settings.

149
00:07:56,856 --> 00:08:01,524
Your tree and rock have deliberate positions, directions, and
sizes.

150
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:03,859
You've saved and walked through the scene.

151
00:08:04,001 --> 00:08:08,020
If a part is unclear, repeat that chapter with this same small
scene.

152
00:08:08,353 --> 00:08:09,799
Keep this lesson save.

153
00:08:09,974 --> 00:08:13,511
In Episode Two, we'll build a woodland landscape with a path,

154
00:08:13,558 --> 00:08:15,568
a focal point, and depth.

155
00:08:15,709 --> 00:08:17,496
Official guide pages are linked in the

156
00:08:17,501 --> 00:08:20,161
description whenever you want to revisit a module.
